# EXPECT: precedes its first module-level binding
#
# Round 57's root-cause probe: module globals are emitted as C globals
# live-at-zero from program start, but the VM's binding exists only from
# its first EXECUTED assignment -- `print of v / v is 1` (no functions
# involved at all) dies rc 1 on the VM and printed 0 here. The top-level
# order guard refuses any top-level use that textually precedes the
# name's first top-level binding statement.
#
# This is the envelope shape (the VM RUNS it, rc 0, prints 1): the use
# sits in a dead if arm, so at runtime the binding always precedes every
# executed use, but statement order cannot prove it. The use scan is
# scope-aware (collect_uses_scoped): for-BODY uses are skipped because a
# name assigned only in a for body is loop-local -- descending would
# false-refuse `for i...: tmp is i / ... / tmp is 99`.

if 1 == 2:
    print of v

v is 1
print of v
